                "entry_id": "dfc0590a-fae8-375f-892b-485b677ce7bd",
                "word": "sunyata",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "859ff83a-c9a4-4326-9c45-15a8d1235fc8",
                        "entry_id": "dfc0590a-fae8-375f-892b-485b677ce7bd",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "sunyatas"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4a5c1521-6370-3fed-b5d9-6ad3fc799a14",
                        "entry_id": "dfc0590a-fae8-375f-892b-485b677ce7bd",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Emptiness, the absence of an intrinsic essence or nature which is stable and separable from other things."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6e73b2e4-fbb3-44a2-a715-84caeb1a04eb",
                                "sense_id": "4a5c1521-6370-3fed-b5d9-6ad3fc799a14",
                                "text": "1993, Sunyata, Emptiness and Self-emptying, Kenosis by Ven. Dr. Rewata Dhamma published in Buddhist - Christian Dialogue London, 27th March\nHowever since emptiness empties itself and so cannot itself be an object of attachment, dynamic 'sunyata' empties itself out as just the things themselves."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "00826c02-abf5-3f5c-80e2-8a00da02f35a",
                        "entry_id": "dfc0590a-fae8-375f-892b-485b677ce7bd",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Emptiness, in terms of a meditative state; an Eastern concept of a high meditative state of calm and freedom from distraction."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "48ed58e7-97e0-4c2c-85ef-60618f21b965",
                                "sense_id": "00826c02-abf5-3f5c-80e2-8a00da02f35a",
                                "text": "1975, Herbert V. Guenther, Chögyam Trungpa, The Dawn of Tantra, 2001, Shambhala Publications, pages not numbered,\nShunyata can be explained in a very simple way. […] In the shunyata experience, the attention is on the field rather than on its contents."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d96a2ff2-09c3-4b12-98bf-060592c42b86",
                                "sense_id": "00826c02-abf5-3f5c-80e2-8a00da02f35a",
                                "text": "1986, White Lotus Sutra - Unchecked by Sangharakshita; STUDY LEADERS SEMINAR 1986 Padmaloka.\nall dharmas were reducible to sunyata. So in a sense you have got a sort of merging of the particular in the universal."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "721aac61-e6e8-4732-b6c1-4755e2391f9c",
                                "sense_id": "00826c02-abf5-3f5c-80e2-8a00da02f35a",
                                "text": "In his treatment of Zen Buddhism, Abe emphasizes that the true self of Zen Buddhism is dynamic śūnyatā: \"That is to say, true Sunyata is nothing but the true self and the true self is nothing but true Sunyata\""
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
